! MCP's EQUIPMENT-CODE LOOKUP, printed p.17 lines 46-54, lifted out
! of the page and run.  The rows, as the listing prints them:
!
!   X134    L(V+I)(BU,12), .24($4)    -SECOND LETTER OF HOLLERITH
!                                      EQUIPMENT
!           LVI, $1, X EQP T          -BASE OF EQUIPMENT TABLE
!           LCI, $1, 10.0             -SIZE OF TABLE +1
!   X135    SIC, X ERR AD
!           CBZ, $1, X ERROR          -WRONG EQUIPMENT CODE
!           KF(V+I)(BU,24), .29($1)   -MATCH * ADVANCE TO NEXT SLOT
!           BZAE, X135
!   X136    L(BU,4), -.5($1)          -ENTER EQUPMENT CODE FROM TABLE
!           ST(BU,4), .52($3)         -INSERT IN CST ENTRY
!
! THE TABLE'S LAYOUT IS DERIVED FROM THESE ROWS, not guessed, and
! this is the point of the deck as much as the execution is.  X EQP T
! itself sits on a page nobody has transcribed, so its CONTENTS are a
! fixture -- but its SHAPE follows from the code that reads it:
!
!   * `KF(V+I)(BU,24), .29($1)` compares TWENTY-FOUR bits and, under
!     progressive indexing, advances $1 by the address field --
!     TWENTY-NINE bits.  So an entry is 29 bits and only its first 24
!     are the comparand.
!   * `L(BU,4), -.5($1)` then reads FOUR bits five back from where
!     the match left $1, i.e. at the entry's bits 24-27.  So the
!     remaining five bits are a four-bit equipment code and one
!     spare.
!
!   entry = [24-bit Hollerith letter][4-bit equipment code][1 spare]
!
! That is the same kind of derivation the PROSA dictionary's shape
! got from printed p.4: the program that reads a structure is a
! primary source for the structure, even when the structure's own
! page is missing.
!
! AND THE LOOP IS A LINEAR SEARCH WITH A COUNTED BOUND.  "LCI,$1,10.0"
! puts ten in $1's count field while "LVI,$1,X EQP T" puts the table's
! address in its value; `CBZ` counts down and takes the error exit
! when the table is exhausted, and `BZAE` goes back for another slot
! while the comparison says not-equal.  Ten entries, the tenth being
! the "+1" the comment names.
!
! WHAT IS CHANGED FROM THE PAGE: the card field and the CST entry are
! read and written at fixed addresses rather than off $4 and $3,
! because this deck has no card image and no channel status table.
! The table walk -- which is the part with the derived layout -- is
! the page's own, progressive indexing included.
!
!   T1  the equipment code lands in the CST entry's bits 52-55.  The
!       table's fourth entry is the matching one, so a search that
!       stopped early, ran away, or took the code from the wrong
!       entry all give a different nibble.
!   T2  the WHOLE index word $1 was left holding, value and count
!       together: the table base plus four entries of 29 bits, and a
!       count of 6 where it started at 10.
!
!       **Checked separately because the code alone cannot tell a
!       correct search from a lucky one** -- the code is read at a
!       NEGATIVE offset from wherever $1 ended, so a walk that
!       advanced by the wrong amount and a compare that matched the
!       wrong entry look the same in T1 and different here.
!
!       AND THE COUNT IS THE BETTER HALF OF IT, which I only noticed
!       by getting the expected value wrong: the first version of
!       this check asserted the value field alone and failed by
!       exactly 6 << 18.  The count says HOW MANY SLOTS WERE
!       EXAMINED -- four, for the fourth entry -- which is the one
!       thing a search that found the right answer the wrong way
!       could not reproduce.

! ONE INJECTION, MEASURED, and the program's own error exit catches
! it: progressive indexing made not to advance (the increment forced
! to zero) leaves the search comparing the first entry ten times
! over, the count runs out, and CBZ takes MCP's "WRONG EQUIPMENT
! CODE" branch.  All three checks move -- the CST entry stays zero,
! $1 is never stored, and the halt is at NOTFOUND rather than PASS.
! A 1963 bootstrap's error path firing for the right reason.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

START   LVI, $X4, CARD              ! (page: $4 already walks the card)
        L(V+I)(BU,12), .24($X4)     ! line 46 -SECOND LETTER OF
                                    !          HOLLERITH EQUIPMENT
        LVI, $X1, EQPT              ! line 47 -BASE OF EQUIPMENT TABLE
        LCI, $X1, 10.0              ! line 48 -SIZE OF TABLE +1
X135    CBZ, $X1, NOTFOUND          ! line 50 -WRONG EQUIPMENT CODE
        KF(V+I)(BU,24), .29($X1)    ! line 51 -MATCH * ADVANCE TO
                                    !          NEXT SLOT
        BZAE, X135                  ! line 52
X136    L(BU,4), -.5($X1)           ! line 53 -ENTER EQUPMENT CODE
                                    !          FROM TABLE
        ST(BU,4), CSTE+.52          ! line 54 -INSERT IN CST ENTRY
        SX, $X1, T2                 ! where the search stopped
        B, PASS

NOTFOUND BEW, $                     ! the page's "WRONG EQUIPMENT CODE"
PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this the fields
! below would straddle words.
        SLC, (8)2030.0

! The card's equipment letter, twelve bits at the field's own start.
CARD    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1257000000000000000000

! Ten entries of 29 bits: twenty-four bits of letter, a four-bit
! equipment code, one spare.  The fourth matches the card.
EQPT    DD(BU,24), 273
        DD(BU,5), 0
        DD(BU,24), 546
        DD(BU,5), 0
        DD(BU,24), 819
        DD(BU,5), 0
        DD(BU,24), 2748
        DD(BU,5), 12
        DD(BU,24), 1092
        DD(BU,5), 0
        DD(BU,24), 1365
        DD(BU,5), 0
        DD(BU,24), 1638
        DD(BU,5), 0
        DD(BU,24), 1911
        DD(BU,5), 0
        DD(BU,24), 2184
        DD(BU,5), 0
        DD(BU,24), 2457
        DD(BU,5), 0

        SLC, (8)2050.0
CSTE    DD(BU,64,8), 0
T2      DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
